  <!-- ============ A NORMAL WORKDAY ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="daily-rhythm">
    <span class="section-label">Getting Started</span>
    <h2>A Normal Workday</h2>
    <p>Once your <a href={url('/project-planning')}>project foundation</a> is in place, daily development follows a simple rhythm. Here's the mental model before diving into each use case.</p>

    <div class="flow"><span class="highlight">Review PRs</span> &rarr; <span class="highlight">Plan features</span> &rarr; <span class="highlight">Build</span> (manual or autonomous) &rarr; <span class="highlight">Monitor dashboard</span> &rarr; <span class="highlight">Wrap up</span>
    <span class="muted">Repeat. Each feature gets its own session and branch.</span></div>

    <p><strong>Morning:</strong> Merge yesterday's PRs. Check <code>/status</code> for open work. <strong>Mid-day:</strong> Build features &mdash; one at a time with <code>/new-feature</code>, or let <code>/run-backlog</code> handle multiple autonomously. <strong>End of day:</strong> <code>/wrap</code> every session, review Vibe Scores, capture stray ideas with <code>/idea</code>.</p>

    <p>The rest of this page covers each part in detail.</p>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ============ PLANNING FEATURES ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="planning-features">
    <span class="section-label">Use Case</span>
    <h2>Planning Features</h2>
    <p><em>"I have ideas and need to turn them into buildable specs."</em></p>

    <h3><code>/idea</code> &mdash; instant capture</h3>
    <p>Ideas come at the worst times &mdash; mid-implementation, during testing, while reviewing code. Capture them instantly without losing flow. One line in, one line out, zero context consumed.</p>

    <pre><code><span class="code-label">any tab</span><span class="prompt">&gt;</span> /idea "add dark mode toggle to settings page"
<span class="output">Added to backlog: "add dark mode toggle to settings page" (unplanned)</span>

<span class="prompt">&gt;</span> /idea "export trip itinerary as PDF"
<span class="output">Added to backlog: "export trip itinerary as PDF" (unplanned)</span>

<span class="prompt">&gt;</span> /idea "rate limiting on auth endpoints"
<span class="output">Added to backlog: "rate limiting on auth endpoints" (unplanned)</span></code></pre>

    <p>Ideas land in <code>.vibecrew/backlog.json</code> with status <code>"unplanned"</code>. They sit there until your next planning session.</p>

    <h3><code>/plan-features</code> &mdash; structured specs</h3>
    <p>When you've accumulated several ideas, run <code>/plan-features</code> to promote them to structured specs. VibeCrew reads your roadmap and walks through each feature. For each one, you provide or confirm:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Name and description</strong> &mdash; one sentence</li>
      <li><strong>Acceptance criteria</strong> &mdash; when is this feature "done"?</li>
      <li><strong>Priority</strong> &mdash; P0 (must have) through P3 (nice to have)</li>
      <li><strong>Complexity</strong> &mdash; S / M / L / XL</li>
      <li><strong>Dependencies</strong> &mdash; which features must exist first?</li>
    </ul>

    <pre><code><span class="code-label">Planning session</span><span class="prompt">$</span> claude
<span class="accent">VibeCrew v1.8.0 | Foundation: complete | No active feature</span>
<span class="output">Ready. 5 features in roadmap, 0 planned.</span>

<span class="prompt">&gt;</span> /plan-features</code></pre>

    <p>The output is <code>.vibecrew/backlog.json</code> &mdash; a structured file that <code>/new-feature</code> and <code>/run-backlog</code> read to determine what to build next.</p>

    <CodeBlock code={backlogJson} lang="json" label="backlog.json (excerpt)" />

    <pre><code><span class="code-label">Planning session</span><span class="prompt">&gt;</span> /wrap
<span class="success">Vibe Score: 92/100</span>
<span class="output">5 features planned. Backlog ready for /new-feature or /run-backlog.</span></code></pre>

    <h3>Weekly planning ritual</h3>
    <p>Every week, ideas accumulate via <code>/idea</code>. Process them before they become a graveyard of forgotten thoughts.</p>

    <div class="flow"><span class="highlight">Every Friday (or Monday morning):</span>

<span class="green">1.</span> Run <span class="green">/status</span> to see the full picture
<span class="green">2.</span> Run <span class="green">/plan-features</span> to promote unplanned ideas to specs
<span class="green">3.</span> Review Vibe Score trends &mdash; are scores going up or down?
<span class="green">4.</span> Review CLAUDE.md rules added by Performance Coach
<span class="green">5.</span> Merge any remaining PRs
<span class="green">6.</span> <span class="green">/wrap</span> to close the week

<span class="muted">Total: ~30 minutes. Sets up the entire next week.</span></div>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ============ BUILDING A SINGLE FEATURE ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="building-a-feature">
    <span class="section-label">Use Case</span>
    <h2>Building a Single Feature</h2>
    <p><em>"I want to build one feature with full control."</em></p>

    <p><code>/new-feature "name"</code> creates a feature branch and initializes 6-phase tracking. Each feature passes through: <strong>Plan &rarr; UI Design &rarr; Code &rarr; Test &rarr; Review &rarr; Docs</strong>. Here's a complete walkthrough building "User Authentication".</p>

    <h3>Starting the feature</h3>
    <pre><code><span class="code-label">Development</span><span class="prompt">$</span> claude
<span class="accent">VibeCrew v1.8.0 | Foundation: complete | No active feature</span>
<span class="output">Next in backlog: user-auth (P0, M complexity)</span>

<span class="prompt">&gt;</span> /new-feature "user-auth"
<span class="output">Created branch: feat/user-auth</span>
<span class="output">Loaded spec from backlog. 6-phase tracking initialized.</span>
<span class="output">Phases: [ ] Plan  [ ] UI Design  [ ] Code  [ ] Test  [ ] Review  [ ] Docs</span></code></pre>

    <h3>The 6 phases</h3>

    <h4>Phase 1: Plan (~3 min)</h4>
    <pre><code><span class="code-label">Development</span><span class="prompt">&gt;</span> "Plan the user authentication feature"

<span class="output">Writing feature spec at docs/features/user-auth.md</span>
<span class="output">  - Email/password signup with Supabase Auth</span>
<span class="output">  - Google OAuth via Supabase provider</span>
<span class="output">  - Middleware for protected routes</span>
<span class="output">  - Session handling with Supabase SSR helpers</span>
<span class="output">  - Edge cases: expired tokens, deleted accounts, rate limiting</span>

<span class="success">Phase complete: Plan</span>
<span class="output">Phases: [x] Plan  [ ] UI Design  [ ] Code  [ ] Test  [ ] Review  [ ] Docs</span></code></pre>

    <h4>Phase 2: UI Design (~5 min)</h4>
    <pre><code><span class="code-label">Development</span><span class="prompt">&gt;</span> "Design the auth UI components"

<span class="output">Designing components using design-system.css tokens:</span>
<span class="output">  - LoginForm: email input, password input, submit, OAuth buttons</span>
<span class="output">  - SignupForm: extends LoginForm with name field, confirm password</span>
<span class="output">  - AuthLayout: centered card layout, responsive breakpoints</span>
<span class="output">  - ProtectedRoute: redirect wrapper component</span>
<span class="output">All colors, spacing, and typography reference CSS custom properties.</span>

<span class="success">Phase complete: UI Design</span>
<span class="output">Phases: [x] Plan  [x] UI Design  [ ] Code  [ ] Test  [ ] Review  [ ] Docs</span></code></pre>

    <h4>Phase 3: Code (~15 min)</h4>
    <p>The longest phase. The Builder agent writes the implementation, consulting Context7 for Supabase and Next.js APIs instead of guessing.</p>
    <pre><code><span class="code-label">Development</span><span class="prompt">&gt;</span> "Implement user authentication"

<span class="output">Builder agent activated. Working within TDR boundaries.</span>
<span class="output">  Checking Context7 for Supabase Auth API...</span>
<span class="output">  Creating src/lib/supabase/client.ts</span>
<span class="output">  Creating src/lib/supabase/server.ts</span>
<span class="output">  Creating src/app/(auth)/login/page.tsx</span>
<span class="output">  Creating src/app/(auth)/signup/page.tsx</span>
<span class="output">  Creating src/middleware.ts</span>
<span class="output">  Creating src/components/auth/LoginForm.tsx</span>
<span class="output">  Creating src/components/auth/SignupForm.tsx</span>
<span class="output">  Creating src/components/auth/OAuthButtons.tsx</span>

<span class="success">Phase complete: Code</span>
<span class="output">Phases: [x] Plan  [x] UI Design  [x] Code  [ ] Test  [ ] Review  [ ] Docs</span></code></pre>

    <Callout type="tip" title="Capturing ideas mid-feature">
      <p>While building auth, you realize you'll need a "forgot password" flow. Don't start building it &mdash; that's scope creep. Capture it instantly with <code>/idea "forgot password flow with email reset link"</code> and get back to work. One line, zero context spent.</p>
    </Callout>

    <h4>Phase 4: Test (~8 min)</h4>
    <pre><code><span class="code-label">Development</span><span class="prompt">&gt;</span> "Write tests for user authentication"

<span class="output">Writing tests (TDD-hybrid approach):</span>
<span class="output">  Creating src/__tests__/auth/login.test.tsx</span>
<span class="output">  Creating src/__tests__/auth/signup.test.tsx</span>
<span class="output">  Creating src/__tests__/auth/middleware.test.ts</span>
<span class="output">  Running test suite...</span>
<span class="success">12 tests passed, 0 failed</span>

<span class="success">Phase complete: Test</span>
<span class="output">Phases: [x] Plan  [x] UI Design  [x] Code  [x] Test  [ ] Review  [ ] Docs</span></code></pre>

    <h4>Phase 5: Review (~3 min)</h4>
    <pre><code><span class="code-label">Development</span><span class="prompt">&gt;</span> /review

<span class="output">Code Reviewer agent activated (read-only worktree).</span>
<span class="output">Reviewing against: feature spec, TDR constraints, project conventions</span>
<span class="output">  Checking TDR compliance... OK</span>
<span class="output">  Checking conventional commit format... OK</span>
<span class="output">  Checking design system token usage... OK</span>
<span class="output">  1 warning: LoginForm missing aria-live region for error messages</span>

<span class="success">Phase complete: Review</span>
<span class="output">Phases: [x] Plan  [x] UI Design  [x] Code  [x] Test  [x] Review  [ ] Docs</span></code></pre>

    <h4>Phase 6: Docs (~2 min)</h4>
    <pre><code><span class="code-label">Development</span><span class="prompt">&gt;</span> "Generate documentation for user auth"

<span class="output">Updating CHANGELOG.md</span>
<span class="output">Updating docs/features/user-auth.md with implementation notes</span>
<span class="output">Session log saved to .vibecrew/sessions/</span>

<span class="success">Phase complete: Docs</span>
<span class="output">Phases: [x] Plan  [x] UI Design  [x] Code  [x] Test  [x] Review  [x] Docs</span></code></pre>

    <h3>Wrapping up</h3>
    <pre><code><span class="code-label">Development</span><span class="prompt">&gt;</span> /wrap

<span class="output">Running quality gate...</span>
<span class="success">  Build: passed</span>
<span class="success">  Tests: 12/12 passed</span>
<span class="success">  Lint: 0 errors</span>
<span class="success">  Types: 0 errors</span>

<span class="output">Performance Coach analysis:</span>
<span class="success">  Vibe Score: 91/100</span>
<span class="output">  Deductions: -5 (1 prompt refinement), -4 (minor context overhead)</span>
<span class="output">  Coaching: "Consider writing test stubs before implementation</span>
<span class="output">   next time to catch edge cases earlier."</span>
<span class="output">  Proposed CLAUDE.md rule: "Write test file stubs with describe</span>
<span class="output">   blocks before implementing features."</span>
<span class="output">  Accept rule? (y/n)</span>

<span class="prompt">&gt;</span> y

<span class="output">Rule added to CLAUDE.md [Session Learnings].</span>
<span class="output">Committed: feat(auth): implement user authentication</span>
<span class="output">Branch feat/user-auth ready for PR.</span></code></pre>

    <Callout type="info" title="Key rules">
      <p><strong>One feature per session.</strong> Each Claude Code session has a finite context window. Cramming two features into one means both get less attention and lower Vibe Scores. <strong>Commit at phase boundaries.</strong> Each phase completion is a natural commit point &mdash; if something goes wrong later, you can roll back to the last clean phase. <strong>Use <code>/check</code> mid-session</strong> to run the full quality gate (build, tests, lint, types) without wrapping up.</p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ============ RUNNING THE BACKLOG ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="run-backlog">
    <span class="section-label">Use Case</span>
    <h2>Running the Backlog Autonomously</h2>
    <p><em>"I want VibeCrew to build multiple features while I do other things."</em></p>

    <p><code>/run-backlog</code> automates the full 6-phase cycle for each feature in your backlog. It picks up the next planned feature, builds it through all phases, wraps it, and moves to the next one.</p>

    <pre><code><span class="code-label">Development</span><span class="prompt">&gt;</span> /run-backlog
<span class="output">Starting autonomous backlog run.</span>
<span class="output">3 features queued: trip-creation, itinerary-builder, group-invites</span>
<span class="output">Building: trip-creation...</span></code></pre>

    <h3>Inter-feature context hygiene</h3>
    <p>Between features, VibeCrew forces a <code>/compact</code> to prevent context degradation across long autonomous runs. Each feature starts with a clean context window, ensuring the Builder agent doesn't confuse Trip Creation's patterns with Group Invites' requirements.</p>

    <h3>When to use <code>/run-backlog</code> vs <code>/new-feature</code></h3>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <h4><code>/run-backlog</code></h4>
        <p>Features are well-specced with clear acceptance criteria. You trust VibeCrew to make implementation decisions. You want to batch-build multiple features while you do other work.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4><code>/new-feature</code></h4>
        <p>The feature needs creative direction or nuanced decisions. You want to guide each phase. You're learning the workflow and want to see each step.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>What to expect</h3>
    <p>VibeCrew notifies you on three conditions only: <strong>permission prompts</strong> (a tool needs approval), <strong>task completion</strong> (a feature finished), and <strong>critical failures</strong> (something broke). Everything else runs silently. You'll get a native OS notification for each &mdash; click it to jump directly to the session.</p>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ============ WORKING IN PARALLEL ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="parallel-work">
    <span class="section-label">Use Case</span>
    <h2>Working in Parallel with Warp</h2>
    <p><em>"I want to plan ahead while a feature builds."</em></p>

    <p>VibeCrew is designed for parallel work across multiple terminal panes. Each project gets its own Warp tab with three split panes.</p>

    <div class="terminal-grid three-col">
      <div class="terminal-window">
        <div class="terminal-titlebar">
          <div class="terminal-dots">
            <div class="terminal-dot red"></div>
            <div class="terminal-dot yellow"></div>
            <div class="terminal-dot green"></div>
          </div>
          <span class="terminal-title">Left &mdash; Planning</span>
        </div>
        <div class="terminal-body"><span class="accent">claude</span>
<span class="output">/plan-features, /idea</span>
<span class="output">/status, /check, /cost</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="terminal-window">
        <div class="terminal-titlebar">
          <div class="terminal-dots">
            <div class="terminal-dot red"></div>
            <div class="terminal-dot yellow"></div>
            <div class="terminal-dot green"></div>
          </div>
          <span class="terminal-title">Right top &mdash; Development</span>
        </div>
        <div class="terminal-body"><span class="accent">claude</span>
<span class="output">Building: user-auth</span>
<span class="output">Code phase active</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="terminal-window">
        <div class="terminal-titlebar">
          <div class="terminal-dots">
            <div class="terminal-dot red"></div>
            <div class="terminal-dot yellow"></div>
            <div class="terminal-dot green"></div>
          </div>
          <span class="terminal-title">Right bottom &mdash; Terminal</span>
        </div>
        <div class="terminal-body"><span class="accent">npm run dev</span>
<span class="output">Local server running</span>
<span class="output">http://localhost:3000</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Left: Planning</h4>
        <p>Your primary focus. <code>/plan-features</code> to refine upcoming specs, <code>/idea</code> to capture thoughts, <code>/status</code> to check progress, <code>/check</code> to run quality gates.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Right top: Development</h4>
        <p>The one in-progress feature moves through the 6 phases here. Guide decisions, review output, run <code>/wrap</code> when done.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Right bottom: Terminal</h4>
        <p>A raw shell for <code>npm run dev</code>, <code>git</code> commands, manual tests, or quick file browsing. Not a Claude Code session.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>How a multi-pane session flows</h3>

    <div class="flow"><span class="highlight">9:00</span>  Development pane: <span class="green">/new-feature "trip-creation"</span>
       Terminal pane: <span class="green">npm run dev</span>

<span class="highlight">9:05</span>  Development: You guide the Plan phase for trip-creation.
       Planning: <span class="green">/plan-features</span> &mdash; refine the itinerary-builder spec.

<span class="highlight">9:25</span>  Development: Code phase starts. Builder agent working.
       Planning: <span class="green">/idea "add map view to itinerary with Mapbox"</span>

<span class="highlight">9:30</span>  <span class="amber">Notification:</span> "Development pane &mdash; Code phase complete."
       You review the code, start the Test phase.

<span class="highlight">9:45</span>  Development: All phases done. <span class="green">/wrap</span> &rarr; Score 88
       <span class="muted">WIP slot is now free.</span>

<span class="highlight">9:46</span>  Development: <span class="green">/new-feature "itinerary-builder"</span>
       Spec is already polished from earlier planning &mdash; Plan phase is fast.

<span class="highlight">10:15</span> Development: <span class="green">/wrap</span> &rarr; Score 85
       Two features shipped in ~75 minutes.</div>

    <Callout type="info" title="WIP limit of 1">
      <p>Only one feature can be in-progress at a time. This is enforced at multiple levels: <code>state.json</code> tracks a single active feature, <code>backlog.json</code> limits the in-progress column to 1, and <code>claim-task.sh</code> blocks new claims. This prevents merge conflicts, keeps context focused, and produces higher Vibe Scores.</p>
    </Callout>

    <Callout type="tip" title="How notifications keep this manageable">
      <p>You don't need to watch all panes. The Interrupt Protocol sends a native OS notification when any session needs you (permission prompt, task complete, or error). Click the notification to jump directly to that pane. Work in your Planning pane until you're interrupted &mdash; that's the whole system.</p>
    </Callout>

    <p>For Warp installation, launch configurations, and keyboard shortcuts, see the <a href={url('/warp')}>Warp Integration</a> page.</p>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ============ MULTI-PROJECT ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="multi-project">
    <span class="section-label">Use Case</span>
    <h2>Multi-Project Development</h2>
    <p><em>"I'm working on 2+ projects simultaneously."</em></p>

    <p>Each project gets its own Warp tab with the same 3-pane layout. Color-code tabs so you can identify projects at a glance.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>1</kbd> through <kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>8</kbd> to jump between projects</li>
      <li><kbd>Opt</kbd>+<kbd>CMD</kbd>+<kbd>Arrow</kbd> to move between panes within a tab</li>
    </ul>

    <Callout type="warn" title="Each pane is an independent Claude Code session">
      <p>Every pane with Claude Code runs its own context window. They share the filesystem (git branches, <code>.vibecrew/</code> state, <code>CLAUDE.md</code>) but not conversation history. This is by design &mdash; planning in one pane doesn't consume context in the development pane. Coordinate between sessions via branches: each feature gets its own branch.</p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ============ MONITORING PERFORMANCE ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="monitoring">
    <span class="section-label">Use Case</span>
    <h2>Monitoring Performance</h2>
    <p><em>"I want to check how my sessions are going."</em></p>

    <h3>Vibe Dashboard</h3>
    <p>A browser-based metrics dashboard showing your Kanban board, Vibe Score trends, and session logbook. Launch it with:</p>

    <pre><code><span class="code-label">Terminal</span><span class="prompt">$</span> bash scripts/start-dashboard.sh</code></pre>

    <p>The dashboard shows feature status (backlog/in-progress/done), score trends over time, and detailed session logs. See the <a href={url('/dashboard')}>Dashboard page</a> for a full tab-by-tab reference.</p>

    <h3><code>/status</code> &mdash; quick CLI check</h3>
    <p>For a quick look without leaving the terminal:</p>

    <pre><code><span class="code-label">any tab</span><span class="prompt">&gt;</span> /status

<span class="accent">Open PRs:</span>
<span class="success">  #12 feat/user-auth        &mdash; approved, ready to merge</span>
<span class="warn">  #13 feat/trip-creation    &mdash; review requested, 2 days old</span>
<span class="output">  #14 feat/itinerary-builder &mdash; draft, tests passing</span>

<span class="output">Context usage: </span><span class="warn">42%</span>
<span class="output">Features: 3/5 complete | Backlog: 2 planned, 4 ideas</span></code></pre>

    <h3>Context budget</h3>
    <p>The <code>check-context.sh</code> hook warns at 45% and 60% context usage. Don't wait for the warning &mdash; check proactively with <code>/status</code>.</p>

    <Callout type="warn" title="What happens at high context usage">
      <p>Above 70%, Claude Code starts losing early conversation context. It may forget design decisions, repeat questions, or generate contradictory code. The rule: <strong>if you're past 60%, wrap up and start fresh</strong>. A new session with full context is always better than pushing a depleted one.</p>
    </Callout>

    <h3>Vibe Score</h3>
    <p>Every session ends with a score (0&ndash;100) calculated from real work artifacts. Starts at 100, applies deductions for prompt churn, tool loops, low cache utilization, missing tests, skipped reviews, and documentation drift. Bonuses for clean sessions, high test coverage, TDD discipline, and complete phase artifacts. The Performance Coach proposes permanent CLAUDE.md rule mutations based on patterns it detects.</p>
  </section>

  <hr>

  <!-- ============ A FULL WEEK ============ -->
  <section class="section" id="full-week">
    <span class="section-label">Putting It Together</span>
    <h2>A Full Week with VibeCrew</h2>
    <p>Here's what a productive week looks like building a travel planning SaaS called <strong>TripFlow</strong> from zero to a working MVP.</p>

    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Monday: Foundation</h4>
        <p><strong>1 tab, 1 session.</strong> Run <code>/new-project</code>. Complete all 6 foundation steps. Run <code>/plan-features</code> to spec out your first 5 features. End with <code>/wrap</code>. Total: ~45 min.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Tuesday: First features</h4>
        <p><strong>1 tab, 2 sessions.</strong> Build user-auth in session 1. Build trip-creation in session 2. <code>/wrap</code> after each. Capture 3 ideas with <code>/idea</code> along the way. Total: ~2 hours.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Wednesday: Full 3-pane workflow</h4>
        <p><strong>3 panes.</strong> Build itinerary-builder in Development while planning group-invites in Planning. Dev server running in Terminal. Once itinerary-builder wraps, start group-invites immediately &mdash; spec is already polished. Total: ~2 hours, 2 features.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Thursday: Final feature + cleanup</h4>
        <p><strong>3 panes.</strong> Development: Build expense-splitting. Planning: Run <code>/simplify</code> on earlier features. Run <code>/heal</code> if CI has issues. Total: ~1.5 hours.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h4>Friday: Polish + review</h4>
        <p><strong>1 tab.</strong> Run <code>/status</code>. Review Vibe Score trends. Plan next week from captured ideas. Review and merge PRs. <code>/wrap</code> to close the week. Total: ~1 hour.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h4>Week summary</h4>
    <div class="flow"><span class="highlight">Features shipped:</span>  5 (user-auth, trip-creation, itinerary-builder, group-invites, expense-splitting)
<span class="highlight">Sessions:</span>           8
<span class="highlight">Average Vibe Score:</span> 89
<span class="highlight">Ideas captured:</span>     12 (ready for next week's /plan-features)
<span class="highlight">CLAUDE.md rules:</span>    3 new rules added by Performance Coach
<span class="highlight">Total active time:</span>  ~8 hours across 5 days</div>

    <Callout type="tip" title="The compounding effect">
      <p>Week 2 is faster than Week 1. The 3 CLAUDE.md rules from this week prevent token waste next week. Your <code>/replay</code> templates capture what worked. The Performance Coach learns your patterns. By Week 4, you're shipping at nearly double the pace &mdash; not because you're working harder, but because the system is eliminating waste automatically.</p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

